WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.220
AI-FMS is an explainable, human-in-the-loop video-review system.

2
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.903
The workbench supports all seven FMS movements, with repetition-
level review

3
00:00:17.923 --> 00:00:20.884
and a movement-specific, pose-based first-pass suggestion.

4
00:00:20.904 --> 00:00:26.721
The AI can explain its evidence and abstain when video quality or
protocol information is incomplete.

5
00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:36.510
A reviewer can isolate complete repetitions, replay a difficult
moment, and correct the timing

6
00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.990
instead of searching through the full video again. The MediaPipe
overlay is aligned to the video,

7
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:50.031
allowing the system to preserve angles, normalized distances,
trajectories, and movement events that are hard to record
consistently by eye.

8
00:00:50.132 --> 00:00:54.824
The AI suggestion supports, rather than replaces, reviewer judgment.

9
00:00:54.844 --> 00:01:00.256
It shows which criteria support a score, what remains uncertain,

10
00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:02.227
and whether protocol information is missing.

11
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:08.285
The reviewer records the final score, confidence, camera view, side,
clearing information,

12
00:01:08.305 --> 00:01:08.882
and notes.

13
00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:15.151
To evaluate the system without leaking AI information, we built a
separate Study Mode.

14
00:01:15.171 --> 00:01:21.160
In both blinded rounds, reviewers could not see AI scores, pose
parameters, source file names,

15
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.160
previous answers, or the other reviewer's decisions.

16
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.036
Reviews were stored as append-only events and exported with
checksums.

17
00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:36.537
Phase I reconstructed 110 repetitions from 28 source videos

18
00:01:36.557 --> 00:01:43.237
and selected 32 across four movements for two blinded rounds.

19
00:01:43.257 --> 00:01:50.937
In Round B, the reviewers agreed on scoreability for all 32 items
and assigned the same score to all 26 jointly scoreable items.

20
00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:57.987
The locked AI exactly matched human consensus on 16 of 25 comparable
items

21
00:01:58.007 --> 00:02:03.707
and was within one point on 23. This is an internal benchmark, not
held-out clinical validation.

22
00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:09.679
The quantitative evidence also revealed what the ordinal score does
not show. Deep Squat formed a strategy continuum;

23
00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:15.779
ASLR preserved side and repeatability differences;

24
00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:21.779
Hurdle Step showed several pathways to the same score;

25
00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:27.749
and Rotary Stability required a full-cycle view of coordination and
event sequence.

